Fix ntoh64() double-swapping the PTP correctionField on big-endian (nqptp 1.2.4).

nqptp reads the IEEE-1588 64-bit correctionField out of a packed struct that
overlays the receive buffer, then passes it through a hand-rolled ntoh64(). That
function was written for a little-endian host: it byte-swaps each 32-bit half
with ntohl() (a no-op on big-endian) and then RE-ASSEMBLES them as
(low<<32)|high. On a big-endian host the whole-uint64 read is already the
correct value, so a correct ntoh64 must be the identity -- instead it transposes
the two 32-bit halves and corrupts the field.

Consequence: when the correctionField is non-zero, handle_follow_up() computes
correctedPreciseOriginTimestamp = preciseOriginTimestamp + correction with a
garbage correction, so the clock offset is nonsense and PTP never converges.
On a flat single-switch subnet the grandmaster's correctionField is normally 0
(and ntoh64(0)==0 either way), so this is latent there -- but it is a real BE
correctness bug in the AirPlay-2 timing path and is removed here so it cannot
bite on a network with transparent-clock switches / boundary clocks / a HomePod
grandmaster reporting residence time.

The rewrite assembles the value from its raw memory bytes in network
(big-endian) order -- the same idiom nqptp's own nctoh64() already uses -- so it
is correct on BOTH little- and big-endian hosts. It produces output identical to
the original on little-endian (no behavior change on upstream's usual targets),
so it is safe to carry downstream and is upstreamable as-is.

--- a/general-utilities.c
+++ b/general-utilities.c
@@ -58,16 +58,19 @@
 }
 
 uint64_t ntoh64(const uint64_t n) {
-  uint64_t fiddle = n;
-  uint32_t fiddle_hi = fiddle & 0xFFFFFFFF;
-  fiddle_hi = ntohl(fiddle_hi);
-  fiddle = fiddle >> 32;
-  uint32_t fiddle_lo = fiddle & 0xFFFFFFFF;
-  fiddle_lo = ntohl(fiddle_lo);
-  fiddle = fiddle_hi;
-  fiddle = fiddle << 32;
-  fiddle = fiddle | fiddle_lo;
-  return fiddle;
+  /* beep-revival: endian-agnostic. The argument holds the 8 wire bytes as read
+     from a packed struct (i.e. a host-order interpretation of big-endian wire
+     data). Reconstruct the value from its raw bytes in network (big-endian)
+     order so this is correct on both little- and big-endian hosts -- the same
+     idiom as the already-correct nctoh64(). The previous implementation was
+     hard-coded for little-endian and transposed the two 32-bit halves on
+     big-endian, corrupting the PTP correctionField. */
+  uint8_t b[8];
+  memcpy(b, &n, sizeof(b));
+  uint64_t v = 0;
+  for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++)
+    v = (v << 8) | b[i];
+  return v;
 }
 
 uint64_t timespec_to_ns(struct timespec *tn) {
